{
  "id": "eks-architecture",
  "name": "EKS architecture",
  "category": "Containers & Kubernetes",
  "difficulty": "Intermediate",
  "description": "EKS control-plane and customer-data-plane architecture based on the supplied diagram. Management arrows are non-traversable illustrations, not application requests. Two EC2 workers in private subnets host three illustrated Ready pods. Start Send Request at Application Service to exercise the bounded EKS workload snapshot. Select More information to edit readiness, selectors and desired replicas. API operations, endpoint reachability, IAM access entries/RBAC, etcd consensus, scheduling, reconciliation, node-to-pod failure propagation and image pulling are NOT simulated. Control-plane boxes represent distributed logical components, not one server each. No ALB/NLB is implied; ClusterIP is internal. The Kubernetes API endpoint is drawn inside the regional managed control-plane group as its access interface, not as an independent global service. Communication arrows show the API server as the hub. The endpoint is its access address, not a worker-management component. Controllers and scheduler watch/update API objects; etcd persists state. Kubelets watch assigned pods and report status via the private endpoint; API server-to-kubelet links illustrate logs, attach and port-forward, not pushing pod scheduling commands.",
  "learningOutcome": "Separate Kubernetes management from application traffic and AWS-managed control plane from customer VPC compute. Verify that desired/running counts do not prove readiness; zero Ready endpoints or a selector mismatch fails. Existing Ready workload paths may survive API unavailability, while new desired state needs reconciliation. Illustrated node placements are snapshots and will not change automatically. Sources: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-architecture.html ; https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/cluster-endpoint.html ; https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/",
